================================================================================================
6. CITATION DRIFT, three documented cases found while reading these sources.
================================================================================================

  Pimentel et al. 2019 -> Collberg & Proebsting
      Pimentel: 'our 24.11% is close to the reproducibility rate of 24.9% that Collberg et al. ach
      ieved'. The TR I read reports 32.3% / 48.3% / 54.0% of 402 code-backed papers; 24.9% is from
       the superseded 2014 version they cite. And the events differ: a C/C++ project BUILDING vs a
       notebook RUNNING.

  Trisovic et al. 2022 -> Chang & Li 2015
      Trisovic: 'They successfully reproduced 33% of the results without contacting the authors an
      d 43% with the authors' assistance.' Chang & Li's own abstract says 29 of 59 = 49% with assi
      stance; 43% is 29/67, a denominator the citing authors substituted silently. Both defensible
      ; neither flagged.

  Samuel & Mietchen 2024, internally
      The abstract's narrative implies 1203/10388 = 11.6%; the Results print 7.61% (1203/15818); t
      he corpus rate is 4.4% (1203/27271). All three are in the same paper and all three are corre
      ct.

  Three cases, all found in one reading pass, in a literature whose subject
  is other people's numerical carelessness. The mechanism is not carelessness:
  it is that a proportion travels without its denominator.

================================================================================================
7. DOES THE POOLING MACHINERY WORK? Three synthetic cases with known answers.
================================================================================================

  (a) homogeneous: every study really is 12%
     replicate 1                                    pooled  11.9%  tau=0.01  I2=19.3%   truth 12.0%
     replicate 2                                    pooled  11.9%  tau=0.02  I2=23.9%   truth 12.0%
     replicate 3                                    pooled  12.0%  tau=0.02  I2=39.1%   truth 12.0%

  (b) genuinely heterogeneous: true rates 4%% to 40%%
     replicate 1                                    pooled  17.0%  tau=0.44  I2=99.8%   truth 18.1%
     replicate 2                                    pooled  17.2%  tau=0.41  I2=99.7%   truth 18.1%
     replicate 3                                    pooled  17.4%  tau=0.41  I2=99.7%   truth 18.1%

  (c) homogeneous truth 12%, but each study prints it over a denominator
      shrunk by a random factor in [1.0, 2.1] -- the multipliers measured in
      section 2. This is the situation I claim the literature is in.
     replicate 1                                    pooled  17.1%  tau=0.24  I2=98.5%   truth 12.0%
     replicate 2                                    pooled  21.1%  tau=0.14  I2=95.6%   truth 12.0%
     replicate 3                                    pooled  17.6%  tau=0.12  I2=94.2%   truth 12.0%

  If (a) sits on the truth with tau near 0, (b) shows a large tau, and (c) is
  biased upward with a spuriously large tau, the machinery is behaving and the
  section-3 contrast between 'as printed' and 'widest' is readable.

================================================================================================
8. HOW MUCH DOES THE BORROWED ICC MATTER?
================================================================================================
  ICC = 0.435 is measured on the Samuel & Mietchen corpus and ASSUMED for
  Trisovic (files in packages) and Pimentel (notebooks in repositories).
  Sweeping it:

     ICC | pooled AS PRINTED [95% CI]         | pooled WIDEST [95% CI]            
   0.000 |  20.6% [ 9.5, 38.9]  tau=0.79      |  11.8% [ 5.3, 24.4]  tau=0.78
   0.200 |  20.6% [10.0, 37.7]  tau=0.75      |  11.9% [ 6.1, 21.9]  tau=0.65
   0.435 |  20.6% [10.2, 37.1]  tau=0.73      |  11.9% [ 6.4, 21.0]  tau=0.60
   0.700 |  20.6% [10.3, 36.8]  tau=0.71      |  11.9% [ 6.6, 20.6]  tau=0.58
   1.000 |  20.6% [10.4, 36.6]  tau=0.70      |  11.9% [ 6.7, 20.4]  tau=0.56

  The point estimates barely move -- clustering widens intervals, it does not
  shift proportions. So the headline contrast (printed ~21%% vs widest ~12%%)
  does NOT depend on the borrowed ICC, and the intervals do.

